This looks like 2022. Headline CPI is 8.22% YoY, which matches the August 2022 print (8.3%) or September (8.2%). Fed funds at 3.08% fits September 2022 (the effective rate was 2.56% in August and 3.08% in September). The 10-year yield at 4.02% is consistent with the end of September 2022, when it was about 3.8–4.0%. Core CPI at 6.66% matches September 2022. The unemployment rate of 3.5% also fits September.

The 1-month US equity return of +8.1% does not fit September, which was down about 9%. October 2022 was up about 8%, so the market data points to October. At the end of October 2022, though, CPI would be the September print of 8.2% and core 6.6%, and the fed funds rate would be 3.08% for October. The 10-year yield at the end of October was about 4.05%, and the 2-year about 4.4%. So the snapshot describes October 2022.

